A||||15||Kasdan||Weight Reduction "Stage 1"||||1||
Z||000000||Kasdan||12-23-2001||09:04 AM||||OK, im in the process of removing the pointless weight adding parts while i get it ready to be painted.  Will removing the front spoiler and metal bracket affect cooling/downforce/handling that much?  it was all bent up in the first place (basicly not functional) and i never had any problems, so i just removed it all together, car looks alot better too!.<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                  <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||12-23-2001||09:32 AM||||That little lip "spoiler" is there for a reason, which is to improve air flow into the radiator. I have heard that removing it will result in engine overheating, summer would be the ultimate test if you want to try it, watch that temp gauge though!||||reg||1
Z||000002||lou_dias||12-23-2001||09:46 AM||||I removed mine on my 87gt 3.4 and I had no problems last year.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroBUZZ||12-23-2001||09:52 AM||||My GT has no lip spoiler and tends to lift at speed ( doesn't over heat).  As my SE does and stays put I always thought that the spoiler was the culprit.  Maybe not.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000004||Kasdan||12-23-2001||10:07 AM||||ok thanks guys, ill leave it off for now!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Smoooooth GT||12-23-2001||12:02 PM||||When I was driving the 87GT I had mine off.. IT did cause the 'floating' problem... I put it back... no more 'floating'... hmm... <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif">                       <BR>      <P><A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A>   <BR>Technical  Support (903) 825-7744 Steve<BR>"Smoooooth GT"<BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||California Kid||12-23-2001||12:26 PM||||As a couple of you have pointed out "front end float" with the spoiler removed. This is probably because the air behind the radiator gets pressured with greater speed (also meaning less flow through radiator). A read an article about this part long ago, can't remember who wrote it, but the part has a necessary design function. It isn't cosmetic, as you can hardly see it anyway. GM would have left it off if they could have gotten away with it.<P>If you're really looking to dump unnecessary weight, the first thing you should get rid of is the factory seats. Get some light weight buckets with foam/vinyl cover.<p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Blacktree||12-23-2001||02:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Will removing the front spoiler and metal bracket affect cooling/downforce/handling that much?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>As mentioned above, removing the front air dam will <I>drastically</I> reduce your front downforce, which in turn will have a negative effect on handling. Even if the air dam is bent, it's still better than nothing.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||johnt671||12-23-2001||04:08 PM||||Read years ago that a front spoiler/air dam starts to provide downforce around 15 MPH or so.  Rear spoilers don't work until around 130 MPH.  Save unneeded weight and forget the rear spoiler unless you can go that fast.||||reg||1
Z||000009||watts||12-23-2001||05:08 PM||||If you REALLY want to save some weight, AND want to say "safety be damned", try removing those massive steel safety beams in your doors.  You can also ditch your spare tire, window squirter bottle, heating system, stereo (speakers, etc), stock seats (already mentioned), carpets and underlay (damn are those things heavy!!), and switch all your glass to lexan.  <P>You weren't planning on driving this on the street anyhow were you?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||blakeinspace||12-23-2001||05:19 PM||||Uhmmm... go on a diet?<P>I have a case of 'dicky-do'<BR>That is where you tummy hang out more than your dicky do.<BR>Barring penile enlargement to circumvent aforementioned problem, I would lay off of beer and that crazy donut thread i read earlier.||||reg||12
Z||000011||fieroX||12-23-2001||06:00 PM||||I plan to eventually remove the saftey beams inside the doors, but replace it with a single piece of 2" tubing for some impact resistance.  I also plan to remove the factory sound deadning material under the carpets and replace it with dynamat.  Im making a custom hood, and trunk out of a single layer of fiberglass cloth, with a special epoxy plastic resin that even paper thin is difficult to break.  Im hoping with the L67 to keep the weight in at about 2600.  2800 with me.  <P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000012||Smoooooth GT||12-23-2001||08:45 PM||||How much do the 'factory' seat's weigh??||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||12-23-2001||10:57 PM||||I had read in a Vette performance magazine that the way they combat the front in float is to have vents from the hood, (exactly as a number of Fieros already do) to keep the air coming through the radiator from having to be forced down. <P>It would make sense to release the preasure especially at high speeds from under the hood. <P>You never said if you were going to be actually putting this thing on the streets or for pure racing. If it is for the streets I would hope that you are careful. We used to have a club member who did the weight reduction route as well and put a 4.3 V6 in on top of that. Sheet aluminum front hood, the first chop top I had ever seen, gutted interior (no dashboard, one seat, nothing but black carpet to cover the chassis)etc etc., unfortunately he hydroplaned it into the freeway center divider twice (first the front end then the back end as he spun) during a sudden and rare rainstorm. He did at least keep the side impact panels, because you had to grab them to close the doors from the inside, if I remember correctly! I believe the car, once the outside was repaired was taken to the FOCOA HQ with their other cars after he took out the 4.3.||||reg||2
Z||000014||Cooter||12-23-2001||11:44 PM||||Don't forget that the front and rear steel bumpers can also be removed for more weight reduction. You will have to create some brackets to hold the bumper covers in place, but it can be done. Just hope that you never hit anything and nothing ever hits you!||||reg||1
Z||000015||Kasdan||12-24-2001||10:45 AM||||Ok guys, some of you asked what im planning on doing with my Fiero....I mostly plan on showing it, and racing it STRICTLY at the strip (Atlanta Dragway).  Hope this clears up any confusion.<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                  <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
